Informational, not a defect: the operating point of a self-heating resistor (thermal
port on its own node) is 1.7e-5 off the analytic value with VACASK's default
tolerances and exact with tight ones.

t1.va: I(p,n) <+ V(p,n)/r*(1 + alpha*Temp(t)); Pwr(t) <+ -V(p,n)^2/r, r = 1k,
alpha = 1, Rth = 1k from t to ground, 1 V source, 1k load. Analytically
u = V(in,out) solves u^3 + 2u - 1 = 0 (u = 0.45339765151640377), V(out) = 1 - u =
0.546602348, V(th) = u^2 = 0.205569430.

Commands (from this directory):
  SIM_TEST=yes PYTHONPATH=<VACASK>/python SIM_MODULE_PATH=<build>/devices vacask test_thermal_own_node_default.sim
  SIM_TEST=yes PYTHONPATH=<VACASK>/python SIM_MODULE_PATH=<build>/devices vacask test_thermal_own_node_tight.sim

Observed (2026-10-25):
  default: V(out) = 0.546593090 (rel 1.69e-05), V(th) = 0.205566219 (rel 1.56e-05);
           the test accepts 1e-3 (VACASK's default reltol) and exits 0
  tight (reltol=1e-12 vntol=1e-15 abstol=1e-18): V(out) = 0.546602348, V(th) = 0.205569430, exit 0
Xyce through PyMS 1.1.0 gives the analytic value to 1e-6 with its default tolerances.
Possible upstream question: whether the default op convergence check should stop
1.7e-5 away on a two-unknown nonlinear loop.
